#{[17592186412969 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412969 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86026" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412969 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412969 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following residue classes modulo $8$ contain the integer $5?$\n\nI. $[-3]_{8}$\n\nII. $[13]_{8}$\n\nIII. $[6]_{8}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412969 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9486abbb-c811-54ba-bf39-16141b4e8ba4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412969 #edb/ref 1074 "We have $5\\in [a]_{8}$ if \n\n$$\n5≡a\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8).\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $5\\in [-3]_{8}$ because $5≡-3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$ $\\,✓$\n\n- $5\\in [13]_{8}$ because $5≡13\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$ $\\,✓$\n\n- $5\\notin [6]_{8}$ because $5≢6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412970 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412970 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86031" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412970 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412970 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following residue classes modulo $7$ contain the integer $-2?$\n\nI. $[1]_{7}$\n\nII. $[5]_{7}$\n\nIII. $[-9]_{7}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412970 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9c32efaa-b6d9-5d28-a23f-80c818eab9ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412970 #edb/ref 1074 "We have $-2\\in [a]_{7}$ if \n\n$$\n-2≡a\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $-2\\notin [1]_{7}$ because $-2≢1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $-2\\in [5]_{7}$ because $-2≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$ $\\,✓$\n\n- $-2\\in [-9]_{7}$ because $-2≡-9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412971 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412971 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78599" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412971 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412971 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following integers belong to the residue class $[9]_{13}?$\n\nI. $4$\n\nII. $-17$\n\nIII. $35$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412971 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a55de8de-5dbf-5007-a3cb-517652d485f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412971 #edb/ref 1074 "The residue class $[9]_{13}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $9$ modulo $13.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[9]_{13} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x=9+13k,\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={9+13k\\,|\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,\\,9-2\\cdot 13,\\,9-13,\\,9,\\,9+13,\\,9+2\\cdot 13,\\,…} \\\\  & ={…,-17,-4,9,22,35,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-17$ and $35$ belong to $[9]_{13},$ while $4$ does not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412972 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412972 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78597" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412972 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412972 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following integers belong to the residue class $[-7]_{11}?$\n\nI. $15$\n\nII. $-4$\n\nIII. $7$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412972 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "abd84654-ac2d-58a6-9574-529f2b4aa6f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412972 #edb/ref 1074 "The residue class $[-7]_{11}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $-7$ modulo $11.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[-7]_{11} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡-7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x=-7+11k,\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={-7+11k\\,|\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,\\,-7-2\\cdot 11,\\,-7-11,\\,-7,\\,-7+11,\\,-7+2\\cdot 11,\\,…} \\\\  & ={…,-29,-18,-7,4,15,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $15$ belongs to $[-7]_{11},$ while $-4$ and $7$ do not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412973 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412973 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412973 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412973 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412973 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412973 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412973 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412973 #edb/ref 1069 "q-214483" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412973 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412973 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $[\\,i\\,]_{223}$ denote the residue class modulo $223$ containing the element $i\\in Z.$ Select the correct options in the statements below.\n\n1. $\\,$ {{field-1}} $[\\,i\\,]_{223}=$ {{field-2}}\n\n2. $\\,$ $[\\,21\\,]_{223}$ {{field-3}} $[\\,162\\,]_{223}=$ {{field-4}}\n\n3. $\\,$ {{field-5}} $={[\\,i\\,]_{223}\\,∣\\,${{field-6}} $\\le ${{field-7}} $<223}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412973 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b83f0a5d-69e2-5527-92eb-107f538fccca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412973 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the residue classes modulo $n$ have the following properties:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ distinct residue classes modulo $n$\n\n- Two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: $[a]_{n}\\cap [b]_{n}=∅$ if $a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- Residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers: \n\n$$\nZ=[0]_{n}\\cup [1]_{n}\\cup ⋯\\cup [n-1]_{n}\n$$\n\n- The integers modulo $n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n{Z}_{n}={[0]_{n},\\,[1]_{n},\\,…,\\,[n-1]_{n}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following properties:\n\n- ${⋃}_{i=0}^{222}\\,[\\,i\\,]_{223}=Z$\n\n- $[\\,21\\,]_{223}\\cap [\\,162\\,]_{223}=∅$\n\n- ${Z}_{223}={[\\,i\\,]_{223}\\,∣\\,0\\le i<223}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412974 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412974 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86033" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412974 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412974 #edb/ref 1071 "If $[a]_{n}$ denotes the residue class containing all integers congruent to $a$ modulo $n,$ then $[16]_{7}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412974 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c46ee0cd-65d1-59a8-92bc-e1fc2e8dbb10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412974 #edb/ref 1074 "We have $[a]_{7}=[b]_{7}$ if \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nThe residue class $[16]_{7}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $17$ modulo $7.$ \n\nWe can find an alternative representation for this residue class as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[16]_{7} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡16\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡2\\cdot 7+2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡2\\cdot 0+2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)} \\\\  & =[2]_{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412975 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412975 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86027" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412975 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412975 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following residue classes modulo $10$ contain the integer $-4?$\n\nI. $[4]_{10}$\n\nII. $[-14]_{10}$\n\nIII. $[6]_{10}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412975 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d1a9f6cf-cd8d-55d6-a82c-4176e7807557" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412975 #edb/ref 1074 "We have $-4\\in [a]_{10}$ if \n\n$$\n-4≡a\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10).\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $-4\\notin [4]_{10}$ because $-4≢4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $-4\\in [-14]_{10}$ because $-4≡-14\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)$ $\\,✓$\n\n- $-4\\in [6]_{10}$ because $-4≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412976 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412976 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86021" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412976 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412976 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following integers belong to the residue class $[7]_{11}?$\n\nI. $18$\n\nII. $-4$\n\nIII. $15$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412976 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e91f8fe9-c6f2-558a-bf39-b0389019d5ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412976 #edb/ref 1074 "The residue class $[7]_{11}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $7$ modulo $11.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[7]_{11} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x=7+11k,\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={7+11k\\,|\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,\\,7-2\\cdot 11,\\,7-11,\\,7,\\,7+11,\\,7+2\\cdot 11,…} \\\\  & ={…,-15,-4,7,18,29,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-4$ and $18$ belong to $[7]_{11},$ while $15$ does not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412977 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412977 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78644" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412977 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412977 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. There are $11$ distinct residue classes modulo $11$\n\nII. $[7]_{5}\\cap [2]_{5}\\ne ∅$\n\nIII. $Z=[0]_{4}\\cup [9]_{4}\\cup [2]_{4}\\cup [15]_{4}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412977 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f68491fc-7fbf-551d-bb56-21bac80c159f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412977 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the residue classes modulo $n$ have the following properties:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ distinct residue classes modulo $n$\n\n- Two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: $[a]_{n}\\cap [b]_{n}=∅$ if $a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- Residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers: \n\n$$\nZ=[0]_{n}\\cup [1]_{n}\\cup ⋯\\cup [n-1]_{n}\n$$\n\n- The integers modulo $n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n{Z}_{n}={[0]_{n},\\,[1]_{n},\\,…,\\,[n-1]_{n}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. There are $11$ distinct residue classes modulo $11.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $7≡2\\,(\\text{mod }5),$ we have that $[7]_{5}=[2]_{5}.$ Therefore, $[7]_{5}\\cap [2]_{5}\\ne ∅.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[9]_{4} & =[1+4\\cdot 2]_{4}=[1]_{4} \\\\ [15]_{4} & =[3+4\\cdot 3]_{4}=[3]_{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0]_{4}\\cup [9]_{4}\\cup [2]_{4}\\cup [15]_{4} & =[0]_{4}\\cup [1]_{4}\\cup [2]_{4}\\cup [3]_{4} \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
